(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608196700")
  (:adr/title "30 of 76 vendored-copy findings came from reading `app.cljc` as a namespace")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-19")
  (:adr/tags "detectors false-positives vendored-copies regex co-scientist")
  (:adr/body
  "## How this started

The SessionStart hook reported **58 NEW** vendored-copy findings in one tick. A
mass-NEW burst usually means the detector's view changed, not the world, so it was
worth reading before acting.

It was neither. The detector had only run twice, four days apart, and 58 was
simply the difference. But reading the findings turned up something else: **37 of
76 said `library repo not checked out here -- provenance unverifiable`**, and the
first ones pointed at `.wt-run/`, `.cloud-itonami-app-adopt/` and
`cloud-itonami-app/data/agent-worktrees/session-.../` -- agent worktrees. That
hypothesis was also wrong: only **3** of the 37 were worktree artifacts. Measuring
twice cost two commands and saved a wrong fix each time.

## The actual defect

`verify-vendored-copies` deliberately parses **prose obligations** -- *keep in sync
with*, *mirror of*, *1:1 port of*, *Faithful port of* -- because (its own comment)
*31 files declare an obligation nothing was checking*. The target is matched with:

    #\"(?:...|[Ff]aithful port of|...)\\s+`?([a-z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*(?:\\.[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*)+)`?\"

**`app.cljc` matches that shape exactly**: lowercase segments joined by a dot. So
*\"Faithful port of app.cljc's b2 read helpers\"* was read as the namespace
`app.cljc`, converted to the path `app/cljc.cljc`, found in no repo, and reported
as a missing checkout -- sending the reader to run `west update` for a repo that
was never missing.

Measured across the tree: **30 of the detector's 76 findings (39%)** were this,
from two names -- `app.cljc` (net-kotobase gateway ports) and `policy.kotoba`.

## Change

A trailing segment from a set of source extensions (`clj cljs cljc edn kotoba js
ts …`) makes the name a file, not a namespace. Those become their own finding:

    warn  file-target:<path>   declares a sync obligation against `app.cljc`, which is a
                               FILE NAME, not a namespace -- nothing can resolve which
                               repo's app.cljc is meant. The obligation is real and
                               unchecked; name the namespace (or add a VENDORED header)
                               to make it checkable

**The obligation is not dropped.** It is real and nothing checks it; what changes
is that the finding now says something a reader can act on, and stops claiming a
repo is missing.

    before   2 pin-dishonest, 37 stale, 37 unresolved  (all fail)
    after    2 pin-dishonest, 37 stale,  7 unresolved  (fail)
                                        30 file-target (warn)

The summary line splits the same way, so `37 UNRESOLVED` no longer folds
*could not find* together with *cannot be resolved by construction*.

## Both directions, in an isolated sandbox

Two copies of one file, differing only in what their header names:

    header says `foo.core`  (a real namespace, upstream committed in a git repo)
      old: stale, drift vs HEAD: 2 line(s)      new: identical -- unaffected
    header says `app.cljc`  (a file name)
      old: fail  unresolved: library repo not checked out here
      new: warn  file-target: ... is a FILE NAME, not a namespace

## Expect churn, once

Thirty findings change key (`unresolved:<path>` -> `file-target:<path>`), so the
next tick will report **30 RESOLVED and 30 NEW**. Nothing regressed and nothing was
fixed by it: the same 30 files, re-diagnosed. Recorded here so the burst is not
read as an event.

## Where this leaves the detector

The 7 remaining `unresolved` are genuine -- a named library that is not checked
out, where `west update --fetch smart <name>` is the right action. The 37 `stale`
are unexamined by this ADR. Vendored copies of `kotoba/datom.cljc` exist in four
repos (`com-etzhayyim-kotoba-datom`, `actor-ibuki`, `tadori`, and the canonical
`kotoba-lang/kotoba-datom`), which is the kind of thing this detector exists to
keep visible.")))
